Z||000007||fierohoho||05-14-2001||10:25 AM||||spideyfoot, last? idea to remove that spark plug.<P>Pick a socket that fits whats left of the plug.<BR>Clean the oil/grease out of the socket and off of the plug,(lacquer thinner works great and leaves no residue).<BR>Mix up a s#+* load of JB weld and glue that socket to the plug (try not to glue it to the head).<BR>Let it dry, (I'd give it double the recomended dry time).<BR>Hopefully that may do it. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||05-14-2001||02:26 PM||||Maybe this is a stupid question?<P>Is the hex part (where your socket grabs) rounded off? If so, how did it get that way? In 10 years of automotive work I never saw that. I have had many stuck and cross threaded but never rounded off. Either way, I have used a Snap-on impact swivel spark plug socket on a 3/8 drive gun for the worst ones. Most times chasing the threads with a spark plug tap is all that's needed to get the new ones back in without trouble. BTW - I have only had to use helicoils and inserts on aluminum heads and that would be on cross threaded ones that totally destroyed the threads.||||reg||5
Z||000009||spideyfoot||05-14-2001||05:43 PM||||I think the socket is really really seized in there. put it this way finially got a good bite with an ezout and the  metal in the ez out snapped  and still no turn on the sparkplug. Ive tried 3 days of soaking/ heating up cooling down, smaller sockets banged on, removing the insulator and ez outing. now what little bit is left still wont budge. Im thinking about running up drill sizes till it hit the thread edge and removing all metal above the threads too. In ten years you have not met anything near the terminal darkness of the black cloud that surrounds me. Heck Ive been bite by a brown recluse spider 3 times in 4 years. Hence spideyfoot. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||thomas_l||05-14-2001||06:57 PM||||I see. The only advice I can give is that impact is the secret to getting stuck bolts loose. That can be a friendly air gun or a hammer and a good chisel. Even a hammer on the bolt while turning or right on the end of the wrench (like on brake lines). Sounds like you been there & done that. I have had a lot of experience getting broken and rusty bolts out but can't help you with the bad mojo - that sort of thing ain't my bag  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000011||spideyfoot||05-14-2001||08:21 PM||||Its out its out. Thanks folks. My idle still sucks but looks like Im running again. If you get in the Nashville area Thomas Ill by you a Happy meal for the inspiration. THANKS||||reg||1
